Q1) A) Describe the Different Types of Distributed Denial
of Service (DDoS) Flooding Attacks. (8 Marks)

1. Definition
A Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) attack is a cyberattack in which multiple
compromised computers or devices send a large amount of traffic to a target
server or network to make its services unavailable to legitimate users.

2. Types of DDoS Flooding Attacks

1. SYN Flood Attack
● The attacker sends a large number of TCP SYN requests to the target
server.
● The server waits for the connection to complete.
● Many incomplete connections consume server resources.
● Genuine users may be unable to establish connections.

2. UDP Flood Attack
● The attacker sends a large number of User Datagram Protocol (UDP)
packets to the target.
● The server spends resources processing the packets and checking for
applications listening on the destination ports.
● This can overload the target system and reduce service availability.

3. ICMP Flood Attack
● The attacker sends a large number of Internet Control Message Protocol
(ICMP) packets, such as ping requests, to the target.
● The target consumes network bandwidth and processing resources
responding to or handling the packets.
● Legitimate network traffic may be delayed or blocked.


4. HTTP Flood Attack
● The attacker sends a large number of HTTP requests to a web server.
● The server attempts to process the requests and generate responses.
● CPU, memory, and application resources may become overloaded.
● The website becomes slow or unavailable


5. DNS Amplification Attack
● The attacker abuses publicly accessible DNS servers to send large
responses toward a victim.
● The victim receives more traffic than the attacker directly sends.
● This can consume the victim's bandwidth and disrupt network services.


6. SYN-ACK or TCP Flooding
● The target receives excessive TCP traffic, including SYN-ACK packets.
● The system must process and filter the incoming packets.
● The resulting traffic can consume network and processing resources.


7. Volumetric Flooding Attack
● The attacker generates a very large volume of traffic from multiple devices.
● The network bandwidth of the victim becomes saturated.


